      "raw": "A URL map is an essential tool for organizing website changes and ensuring smooth transitions between old and new addresses. Begin by listing all current URLs alongside their intended replacements in a structured format, such as columns for the old URL, planned new URL, and any observed test results. This visual representation helps track progress and identify discrepancies early on.\n\nTesting redirects is crucial to verify that each old address successfully directs users to its designated new page. Conduct these tests individually—first check the redirection from the old URL to the planned destination, then confirm that visitors land on the actual content of the new page. Remember, a successful map does not guarantee ranking outcomes or traffic improvements; it merely confirms functionality.\n\nWhen testing fails, document the issue rather than marking it as completed. This approach allows for follow-up adjustments and ensures no potential problems are overlooked. By maintaining an updated URL map with clear test results, you can efficiently manage website migrations while keeping a record of any unresolved redirects for future review.",
